    },
    "description": "Cedar Travel exposes account, itinerary, and refund operations through an API used by its mobile application and partner integrations. Login currently establishes a role claim that many controllers trust for the remainder of the request, and several methods repeat slightly different ownership checks. The Platform Security team will separate business authorization from the resource services. An Authentication Filter will establish the subject and evidence, a Policy Service will evaluate rules based on subject, operation, tenant, and resource attributes, and an Authorization Filter will require a decision before every controller invocation. The Invoice aggregate, Refund service, and Account repository may no longer infer permission directly from role fields inside their methods. The flow must include normal requests, retries, batch exports, support impersonation, and maintenance endpoints, with each invocation receiving a fresh decision so a revoked role or transferred ownership is not honored from an earlier check. Policy changes must be reviewable and testable without changing aggregate code, and denial responses must avoid revealing inaccessible records. Service-to-service calls must carry verifiable subject evidence rather than an untrusted customer identifier header.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide whether business authorization should be centralized in a separately owned policy decision service and required before every protected operation.",
    "notes": "Business authorization moves to a separately owned Policy Service enforced before every operation (policy-guard); complete-mediation is the near-miss via fresh per-invocation decisions on all standard and nonstandard paths."
  },
